## Why

Interference from a first language is often grammatically defensible — it
isn't wrong the way a typo is wrong, it's *foreign*, in ways a native reader
notices as friction even when they can't immediately name what's off. A
screening pass exists so that friction gets mapped before the detailed edit
starts, rather than discovered piecemeal sentence by sentence.

The risk this pass runs is the same one the ESL audit guards against, in a
sharper form: a model told to hunt for "patterns caused by the author's native
language" will find them everywhere, because almost any stylistic choice can
be *attributed* to L1 influence in hindsight. Flagging phrasing that is
already natural, idiomatic English — merely because it happens to differ from
what the author might produce in their L1 — wastes the downstream editor's
time and teaches them to distrust the flagged list. The value of this pass is
in precision: a short, accurate diagnostic that the full edit can act on, not
an exhaustive list of everything that could conceivably be interference.

