### Scope

- This pass identifies and categorises; it does not rewrite the document or
  produce final corrected text. The `Suggestion` column offers a plausible
  fix for diagnostic clarity, not a ready-to-apply edit.
- Categories are fixed to the eight listed in *Reference — Interference
  categories*. Don't invent new categories; if a pattern doesn't fit any of
  the eight, note it in the summary's dominant patterns rather than forcing
  it into a table row.
- Register and terminology correctness that isn't interference-driven is out
  of scope for this pass — leave it for the full language edit.

### When to stop and ask

- **Author's native language(s) not specified — ask before doing anything
  else.** This is the mandatory first step of every run (see Job). Different
  native languages produce structurally different interference signatures —
  a Nguni language (e.g. Zulu, Xhosa) and a Germanic one (e.g. Afrikaans) will
  surface different patterns even in the same category — so scanning cannot
  meaningfully begin without this answer. Ask directly (e.g. "Which native
  language(s) should I screen this document against?") and wait; do not
  infer the language from the text itself, and do not default to any
  particular language.
- **Multiple native languages given.** If more than one native language is
  specified for a single document, ask whether to screen against each
  independently (and note which language a given flag is attributed to) or
  treat them as a combined profile, unless this is already made clear.
- Proceed without asking on everything else — the interference categories,
  the severity bands, and the output format are settled by this prompt.

